`),marks:5}]})),Cae=le("economics/2025/q25",()=>({question:["Explain the reasons for trends since 2016 in the size and composition of Australia's Balance of Payments. In your answer, refer to the information provided.","","The COVID-19 recession and associated international border closures resulted in a sharp decline in education and tourism service exports, but exports of these services have grown strongly in recent years following the re-opening of the border. The composition of Australia's capital flows has also changed significantly in the past decade or so. Unlike in the past, Australia's economy since 2017 has generated savings in excess of domestic investment.","",See()],sampleAnswer:["Since 2016, the size and composition of Australia's Balance of Payments have shifted toward stronger current account outcomes. The graph shows the current account balance moving from deficit toward surplus, mainly because the net goods and services balance improved while the net primary income balance remained negative.","A key reason was stronger export performance, especially in goods, supported by favourable commodity prices and demand for Australian resource exports. Although border closures during COVID-19 sharply reduced service exports such as tourism and education, those exports recovered as international travel resumed. This changed the composition of the current account over time, with services first weakening and then improving again after reopening.","Australia's savings-investment position also changed. Since 2017, domestic saving has been relatively strong compared with domestic investment, reducing the need for foreign borrowing. That contributed to changes in the financial account and the composition of capital flows compared with earlier periods when Australia relied more heavily on net capital inflow.","The persistent net primary income deficit reflects income paid to foreign owners of Australian assets, so even with stronger trade performance this part of the current account remained negative. Overall, movements in export earnings, service-trade disruptions and recovery, and changes in the saving-investment gap explain the recent trends in both the size and composition of Australia's Balance of Payments."].join(`
