`),sampleAnswer:["## Geiger–Marsden Experiment","","Geiger and Marsden fired alpha particles at a thin gold foil. They observed:","- Most alpha particles passed straight through.","- A small fraction were deflected at large angles.","- A very small number (about 1 in 8000) bounced almost straight back.","","The **Rutherford nuclear model** explains these results:","- The atom is mostly **empty space**, so most alphas pass through undeflected.","- A tiny, dense, positively charged **nucleus** at the centre contains almost","  all the mass.","- The rare large-angle deflections occur when an alpha particle comes very","  close to the nucleus; the strong **electrostatic (Coulomb) repulsion** between","  the positive nucleus and positive alpha particle deflects it sharply.","","*(Diagram: atom with small nucleus at centre; arrows showing most alphas","passing through, a few slightly deflected, one bouncing back.)*","","## Hydrogen Emission Spectrum","","Bohr extended Rutherford's model by proposing that electrons orbit the nucleus","in **fixed circular orbits** (stationary states) with specific quantised energies.","","**Forces:** The electrostatic (Coulomb) attraction between the negative electron","and positive nucleus provides the centripetal force for circular orbit:","$$F = \\frac{ke^2}{r^2} = \\frac{mv^2}{r}$$","","**Quantised energy levels:** For hydrogen, the energy of level $n$ is:","$$E_n = -\\frac{13.6}{n^2} \\ \\text{eV}$$","","Electrons can only exist in these allowed levels; they cannot spiral inward","(addressing the classical instability problem).","","**Photon emission:** When an electron transitions from a higher energy level","$n_i$ to a lower level $n_f$, it emits a **photon** with energy equal to","the difference:","$$E_{\\text{photon}} = hf = E_{n_i} - E_{n_f}$$","","**Example calculation** (Balmer series, $n = 3 \\to n = 2$):","$$E_{\\text{photon}} = \\left(-\\frac{13.6}{9}\\right) - \\left(-\\frac{13.6}{4}\\right)","= -1.51 - (-3.40) = 1.89 \\ \\text{eV}$$","","$$f = \\frac{E_{\\text{photon}}}{h} = \\frac{1.89 \\times 1.602 \\times 10^{-19}}","{6.626 \\times 10^{-34}} \\approx 4.57 \\times 10^{14} \\ \\text{Hz}$$","","This corresponds to red light ($\\lambda \\approx 656$ nm), matching the observed","H$\\alpha$ line in the hydrogen spectrum.","","Since only specific energy differences are allowed, only specific frequencies","of light are emitted, producing the **discrete line spectrum** of hydrogen.","","## Limitations","","While the Bohr–Rutherford model successfully explains the hydrogen spectrum","and Geiger–Marsden results, it cannot explain multi-electron atoms, the","relative intensities of spectral lines, or fine structure — these require","quantum mechanics."].join(`
