`),sampleAnswer:["In both models, the projectile starts with the same initial kinetic energy.","As it rises, kinetic energy is converted to gravitational potential energy.","At maximum height, all kinetic energy has been converted to potential energy.","","**Model A (uniform field):**","The gravitational field strength $g$ is constant at all altitudes.","For each metre the projectile rises, the same amount of kinetic energy","is converted to gravitational potential energy ($\\Delta E_p = mg\\Delta h$).","","**Model B (radial field):**","The gravitational field strength $g$ **decreases** with increasing distance","from Earth's centre ($g \\propto 1/r^2$).","As the projectile rises, the gravitational field weakens, so **less kinetic","energy is converted per metre** of height gained.","","**Comparison:**","Since the same total kinetic energy must be converted to potential energy in","both models, but Model B requires less energy per metre at higher altitudes,","the projectile in **Model B reaches a greater maximum height** than in Model A."].join(`
