`),sampleAnswer:["**Early observations — evidence that cathode rays exist**","","In the 1850s–1860s, Plücker and Hittorf observed that evacuated discharge","tubes produced glowing fluorescence opposite the cathode, with sharp shadows","cast by objects placed in the tube. This showed that rays travelled in","straight lines from the cathode.","","**Crookes — charge and momentum**","","William Crookes (1870s–80s) demonstrated that a magnetic field deflected","the rays in the direction expected for a negative charge, and that a","lightweight paddlewheel inside the tube was made to rotate — showing the","rays carried both momentum and (by implication) mass. These results","suggested cathode rays were negatively charged particles, not merely","electromagnetic waves.","","**Perrin (1895) — direct charge measurement**","","Jean Perrin collected cathode rays in a Faraday cup and detected a net","negative charge, providing direct experimental confirmation that the rays","carried negative charge.","","**J.J. Thomson (1897) — identification of the electron**","","Thomson balanced crossed electric ($E$) and magnetic ($B$) fields to","select particles with speed $v = E/B$, then used the electric deflection","alone to measure the charge-to-mass ratio:","","$$\\frac{q}{m} \\approx 1.76 \\times 10^{11} \\text{ C kg}^{-1}$$","","Crucially, this value was the **same** regardless of the cathode material","or the gas in the tube. Thomson concluded that cathode rays consist of","identical sub-atomic particles — later called **electrons** — that are a","universal constituent of all matter.","","**Assessment**","","The cathode ray experiments provided progressive and decisive contributions:","early experiments established that the rays carried negative charge and","had particle-like properties; Thomson's quantitative measurements definitively","identified a new fundamental particle with a specific charge-to-mass ratio.","Together, these experiments moved physics from a descriptive understanding","of electrical discharge to a quantitative model of sub-atomic structure,","forming the foundation for the modern understanding of the electron."].join(`
`),marks:1}}const YJe=1,VJe={id:"physics/2025/q31",version:1,variants:YJe,generate:WJe};function XJe(t){return{question:["Analyse the consequences of the theory of special relativity in relation","to length, time and motion. Support your answer with reference to","experimental evidence."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["**Postulates of special relativity**","","Einstein's 1905 theory rests on two postulates:","1. The laws of physics are identical in all inertial (non-accelerating)","   reference frames.","2. The speed of light in a vacuum, $c$, is the same for all observers,","   regardless of the motion of the source or observer.","","These postulates have profound consequences for measurements of length,","time, and mass when relative speeds are a significant fraction of $c$.","","**Time dilation**","","A clock moving at speed $v$ relative to an observer runs more slowly.","If the clock measures a proper time $t_0$ (time in its own rest frame),","the observer measures a longer time $t$:","","$$t = \\gamma\\, t_0, \\qquad \\gamma = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - v^2/c^2}}$$","","Since $\\gamma \\geq 1$, moving clocks always run slow relative to a","stationary observer.","","*Experimental evidence — atmospheric muons:*","Muons produced by cosmic rays at ~15 km altitude travel at $v \\approx 0.998c$.","Their rest-frame half-life is $\\approx 1.56\\,\\mu$s; classically they should","travel only ~0.5 km before decaying. Yet they are detected in significant","numbers at Earth's surface. Time dilation (from Earth's frame) or length","contraction (from the muon's frame) fully accounts for the observation.","","*Experimental evidence — Hafele–Keating (1971):*","Caesium atomic clocks flown around the world in aircraft were compared to","ground-based clocks. The differences in elapsed time matched the combined","predictions of special and general relativity to within experimental uncertainty.","","**Length contraction**","","An object of rest length $L_0$ moving at speed $v$ parallel to the direction","of motion appears shortened to an observer at rest:","","$$L = \\frac{L_0}{\\gamma} = L_0\\sqrt{1 - \\frac{v^2}{c^2}}$$","","The contraction only occurs in the direction of motion.","From the muon's reference frame, the atmosphere appears contracted to","$\\approx 0.5$ km, allowing the muon to reach the surface before decaying.","","**Consequences for motion and relativistic mass**","","The relativistic momentum of an object is $p = \\gamma m_0 v$, and its","total energy is $E = \\gamma m_0 c^2$. As $v \\to c$, both $\\gamma$ and the","energy required to accelerate the object approach infinity. Therefore,","**no object with rest mass can reach or exceed the speed of light**.","","*Experimental evidence — particle accelerators:*","Protons and electrons accelerated in synchrotrons exhibit the relativistic","mass increase. The magnetic rigidity required to bend the particles at high","energies (and the power needed to accelerate them) are precisely consistent","with $m = \\gamma m_0$, providing ongoing confirmation of special relativity.","","**Summary**","","Special relativity predicts that time, length, and momentum are all","frame-dependent quantities. These consequences — once considered radical —","are now routinely confirmed in particle physics, astrophysics (muon","detection), precision timekeeping (GPS and atomic clocks), and accelerator","design."].join(`
`),marks:1}}const GJe=1,UJe={id:"physics/2025/q32",version:1,variants:GJe,generate:XJe};function QJe(t){return{question:["Analyse the role of experimental evidence and theoretical ideas in","developing the Standard Model of matter."].join(`
`),sampleAnswer:["**Theoretical foundations**","","The Standard Model describes matter in terms of two groups of fundamental","particles — **quarks** (which make up hadrons such as protons and neutrons)","and **leptons** (such as electrons and neutrinos) — and the force-carrying","bosons (photons, W, Z, gluons, and the Higgs).","","The quark model was proposed independently by Murray Gell-Mann and George",'Zweig in 1964 to explain the observed patterns (the "Eightfold Way") of',"hadron masses and quantum numbers. Gell-Mann's theoretical framework","predicted the existence of the then-undiscovered omega-minus baryon, which","was subsequently confirmed experimentally.","","In the 1960s–70s, Glashow, Salam and Weinberg developed the electroweak","theory, mathematically unifying the electromagnetic and weak nuclear forces.","The theory predicted the existence of massive **W$^\\pm$** and **Z$^0$**","bosons as force carriers of the weak interaction.","","**Experimental evidence confirming the Standard Model**","","*Deep inelastic scattering (SLAC, 1968–69):*","High-energy electrons were scattered off protons. The large-angle scattering","observed was inconsistent with a smooth charge distribution, indicating","point-like, hard scatterers inside the proton — the first direct experimental","evidence for quarks as physical constituents of matter.","","*Discovery of W and Z bosons (CERN, 1983):*","The UA1 and UA2 experiments at CERN's proton–antiproton collider detected","the W$^\\pm$ and Z$^0$ bosons at the masses predicted by electroweak theory.","This was a decisive confirmation of the theoretical unification and earned","Rubbia and van der Meer the 1984 Nobel Prize.","","*Higgs boson discovery (CERN LHC, 2012):*","The Higgs field was proposed in 1964 as the mechanism by which particles","acquire mass via spontaneous symmetry breaking. After decades of searching,","the ATLAS and CMS experiments at the Large Hadron Collider announced the","discovery of a particle consistent with the Higgs boson at a mass of","~125 GeV, completing the particle content of the Standard Model.","","**Role of interplay between theory and experiment**","","The Standard Model exemplifies the iterative relationship between theory","and experiment in physics: theoretical predictions prompted the design of","experiments (e.g., the hunt for W/Z bosons and the Higgs), while unexpected","experimental results (e.g., deep inelastic scattering) motivated new","theoretical frameworks (quarks, QCD). Advances in accelerator technology","were essential to reaching the energy scales needed to probe sub-nuclear","structure, allowing theory and experiment to converge on a unified model","of fundamental particles and forces."].join(`
