`)],sampleAnswer:["**Applying conservation of momentum to find $v_b$**","","Let the total satellite mass be $m$, so $m_a = m_b = \\dfrac{m}{2}$.","","Before the explosion, the satellite moves tangentially at speed $v$:","$$p_{\\text{before}} = mv$$","","After the explosion, applying conservation of momentum (no external","tangential impulse during the instantaneous explosion):","$$mv = \\frac{m}{2}(2v) + \\frac{m}{2}v_b$$","$$mv = mv + \\frac{m}{2}v_b$$","$$v_b = 0$$","","Piece $m_b$ has **zero velocity** immediately after the explosion.","","**Motion of $m_a$ (speed = $2v$ at geostationary radius $r$)**","","The geostationary orbital speed is $v = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{GM}{r}}$, so the","escape speed from the same radius is:","$$v_{\\text{esc}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2GM}{r}} = \\sqrt{2}\\,v \\approx 1.41\\,v$$","","Since $2v > 1.41v$, the speed of $m_a$ exceeds the escape velocity.","$m_a$ will follow a **hyperbolic trajectory** and escape Earth's","gravitational field permanently.","","**Motion of $m_b$ (speed = $0$ at geostationary radius $r$)**","","With zero velocity, $m_b$ has no centripetal support. Gravity acts","radially inward (toward Earth). $m_b$ will fall toward Earth under","gravity in a **straight radial line** (a degenerate ellipse with","semi-major axis $= r/2$), striking Earth's surface after one half of",'the "orbit" period.',"","**Summary**","","| Piece | Velocity after | Outcome |","|-------|---------------|---------|","| $m_a$ | $2v$ (tangential) | Exceeds escape velocity → hyperbolic escape trajectory |","| $m_b$ | $0$ | Falls radially inward → collides with Earth |"].join(`
